<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaʻieʻiewaho Channel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kauai Channel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kaʻieʻiewaho Channel separates the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and is about 115 kilometres wide, the widest channel between the main Hawaiian Islands. Its rough waters protected Kauaʻi from invasion: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>'s attempts to cross with large fleets in 1796 and 1804 were turned back by a storm and an epidemic. Kauaʻi joined his kingdom by agreement in 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaieiewaho-channel.jpg" alt="Kaʻieʻiewaho Channel" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Houdinipeter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Channels-of-the-Hawaiian-Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
